INT. SCRAPYARD OFFICE - NIGHT

A trailer on cinder blocks. A single bulb. A safe with its door hanging open and empty.

ROLAND FAYE, 50s, scrapyard owner, sits behind the desk with his hands flat on the blotter. Across from him, NINA SZABO, 30s, leather jacket, a revolver on her knee, sits very straight.

                    ROLAND
You were my best driver.

                    NINA
That's why you sent a stranger to my house?

                    ROLAND
I didn't send anyone.

                    NINA
He knew my dog's name.

                    ROLAND
Everybody knows your dog's name.

                    NINA
You sold me out for sixty thousand.

                    ROLAND
I sold you out for a roof.

                    NINA
Same thing, Roland.

                    ROLAND
You never had to build a business from a hole in the ground.

                    NINA
I've been in the hole. I know what it costs.

The trailer door opens behind her.

DUKE HARTWELL, 40s, fedora, wet shoulders, one hand in his coat, leans in the frame. He does not smile.

                    DUKE
Evening.

Nina doesn't turn. Her thumb finds the hammer.

                    NINA
You're late.

                    DUKE
Traffic.

                    ROLAND
Duke, don't.

                    DUKE
Sorry, Roland. The bill's come due.
